WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.821
Ownership fundamentals are a practical
design habit.

2
00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:05.878
Start by asking who owns each value and
who is responsible for cleanup.

3
00:00:05.958 --> 00:00:09.287
If a function needs long-term
responsibility, ownership may be appropriate.

4
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.142
If it only needs temporary access,
borrowing is usually clearer.

5
00:00:12.222 --> 00:00:14.594
The signature should communicate the
responsibility.

6
00:00:14.674 --> 00:00:16.238
Borrow when ownership is not needed.

7
00:00:16.318 --> 00:00:19.799
Use shared references for reading and
mutable references for controlled change.

8
00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:21.544
Keep mutation explicit and exclusive.

9
00:00:21.624 --> 00:00:23.037
When the borrow checker objects,

10
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:25.841
treat that feedback as a signal that
scopes, responsibilities,

11
00:00:25.921 --> 00:00:27.384
or data flow may need to be clearer.

12
00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:29.836
Use lifetimes to understand reference
relationships.

13
00:00:29.916 --> 00:00:31.530
Most lifetime reasoning is inferred,

14
00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.839
but the concept matters every time a
function returns a reference or a struct

15
00:00:34.919 --> 00:00:35.525
borrows data.

16
00:00:35.605 --> 00:00:39.136
If a borrowing relationship becomes hard
to express, consider whether owned data,

17
00:00:39.216 --> 00:00:40.478
a different state structure,

18
00:00:40.558 --> 00:00:43.686
or a clearer API would better match the
program's real responsibilities.

19
00:00:43.766 --> 00:00:46.642
Design functions, structs, and enums
around ownership contracts.

20
00:00:46.722 --> 00:00:50.405
Choose strings, slices, collections,
iterators, and smart pointers intentionally.

21
00:00:50.485 --> 00:00:52.604
Avoid clones that only hide ownership
confusion,

22
00:00:52.684 --> 00:00:56.114
but do not reject owned data when it makes
the design safer and easier to maintain.

23
00:00:56.194 --> 00:00:57.809
Ownership is not a hurdle to overcome.

24
00:00:57.889 --> 00:00:59.806
It is how Rust makes responsibility
visible.

25
00:00:59.886 --> 00:01:02.661
When used well, it supports memory safety,
predictable cleanup,

26
00:01:02.741 --> 00:01:04.356
clear APIs, concurrency discipline,

27
00:01:04.436 --> 00:01:07.412
and reviewable data flow across
applications, libraries, services,

28
00:01:07.492 --> 00:01:09.460
command-line tools, and systems
components.
